From 1f634331afb2b35f7d50dcf5b0b71b20d9b7f158 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 08:18:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/20] feat(deployment): stop instances a killed control plane
 left running

A control plane that runs directly on a host and is killed with
SIGKILL (or crashes, or is OOM-killed) leaves its vllm-process and
tgi-process instances running in their own sessions, still holding
their GPUs. After the restart the launcher no longer knows them, so
the services it redeploys compete with them for the same devices.

The process launchers now record every instance in a JSON file next
to the database (forgemesh.instances.json): instance id and key, the
pid (also its process group and session), the start time from /proc
and its private directories, together with the boot id and the PID
namespace. A new RuntimeLauncher.start hook, which the app calls
before it resumes unfinished deployments, stops the recorded
instances that are still running the way stop does, then removes
their directories and records.

Only a process whose pid and start time both match its record is
signalled, and records from another boot or PID namespace are
ignored, so a reused pid is never hit. A leftover that cannot be
stopped keeps its record for the next start; a record that cannot be
written fails the launch and stops its process. PR_SET_PDEATHSIG is
not used: it reaches only the instance process, not the process
groups TGI starts, and Python can set it only from preexec_fn, which
is unsafe in the threaded control plane.

The SIGKILL loop now takes the leader's liveness and a wait function,
so it serves both the launcher's own children and leftovers that are
no longer its children.
